/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"note-level-3","number":3,"title":"Set Settings","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"3.1","modules":[{"id":"note-m-3-1-101","code":"3.1","title":"3.1","blurb":"3.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l3-1-1","title":"3.1.1 — Open Set Settings","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.1 — Open Set Settings","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.1 — Metronome</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can open Set Settings from an open Set and list every page that lives there."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To access Set-specific settings, tap the <strong>Set Settings</strong> icon in the upper right corner of a Note Set. It sits to the right of the Set name. This is the same door you located in Lesson 2.1.3. Level 3 lives inside that menu."},{"type":"prose","html":"The manual lists four items: <strong>Metronome</strong>, <strong>Tempo</strong>, <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>, and <strong>Export Audio</strong>. The FAQ adds <strong>Groove Amount</strong> in the same top-right menu (Note 1.0.15+). If your Help version is older, Groove Amount is absent."},{"type":"prose","html":"This menu is not App Settings. App Settings open from the three dots in the Sets Library (Cloud, Link, Help, Live Lite). Set Settings belong to the Set you have open. Tempo, click, key, groove, and bounce are per Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Export Audio</strong> is greyed out when the Set has no clips. You will confirm that at the end of this level. For now, open the menu and learn the list."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set so you are in Session View.","Tap the icon to the right of the Set name (<strong>Set Settings</strong>).","Read the list. Point at <strong>Metronome</strong>, <strong>Tempo</strong>, <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>, and <strong>Export Audio</strong>. On 1.0.15+, also point at <strong>Groove Amount</strong>.","Close the menu. Open it again from memory.","Recite the Set Settings list and the rule: this menu is per Set, not App Settings."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is Set Settings? <strong>Answer:</strong> Upper right of the Set, to the right of the Set name.","Name the four items the manual lists there. <strong>Answer:</strong> Metronome, Tempo, Key &amp; Scale, Export Audio.","Which extra item does the FAQ add on 1.0.15+? <strong>Answer:</strong> Groove Amount."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5 Set Settings (icon, upper right)","Ableton Note Manual §4 intro (tempo, metronome, key and scale, export)","Note FAQ p.4: Set Settings for tempo, Groove Amount, Key and Scale","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Global Groove Amount"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Set Settings five times. Each time, say which item you would use for click, BPM, scale, groove, and bounce."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-1-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-1-2","title":"3.1.2 — Metronome switch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.2 — Metronome switch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.1 — Metronome</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can turn the metronome switch on and off in Set Settings."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The easiest way to record in sync with a set tempo is to play along with the built-in metronome. That is the manual's reason for the click. The control is a switch in <strong>Set Settings</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Enable the switch to arm the click. Disable it to silence the click. The switch alone does not start time. The next lesson covers when the metronome actually ticks: after the switch is on, you press <strong>Play</strong> or launch a clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+, an empty Set no longer turns the metronome on automatically; a count-in plays instead. That is a later lesson. On the pre-2.0 surface, you still own the switch. Do not assume a new Set has the click on or off until you look."},{"type":"prose","html":"The metronome is per Set. It is not an App Settings preference. A Set you used with the click on still has that switch state when you reopen it, unless you changed it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings</strong>.","Find the <strong>Metronome</strong> switch. Turn it on. Close the menu.","Open <strong>Set Settings</strong> again and confirm the switch is still on.","Turn it off. Confirm it stays off when you reopen the menu.","Recite: metronome is a Set Settings switch; it arms the click, it does not by itself start transport."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the metronome switch? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Set Settings.","Why does the manual say to use it? <strong>Answer:</strong> It is the easiest way to record in sync with a set tempo.","Does turning the switch on start the click immediately? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The metronome starts ticking when the switch is on and you press Play or launch a clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.1 Metronome (switch in Set Settings; play in sync with a set tempo)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: empty Set no longer auto-enables metronome (next lesson)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave the switch on for the next lesson. You will hear when it actually starts."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-1-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-1-3","title":"3.1.3 — Metronome starts with Play or clip launch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.3 — Metronome starts with Play or clip launch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.1 — Metronome</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can start the metronome by arming its switch and then pressing Play or launching a clip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The metronome will start ticking when you enable its switch in Set Settings, then press the <strong>Play</strong> button or launch a clip. Both halves are required: switch on, then transport."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the switch is off, Play and clip launch run the Set without a click. If the switch is on and transport is stopped, you should not hear a continuous click sitting there — the manual ties the tick to Play or clip launch."},{"type":"prose","html":"Clip launch still waits for the nearest downbeat. The click follows the Set tempo (the Tempo page in the same menu). If the tempo is wrong, the click is wrong. Fix tempo in Module 3.2. Do not fight the click by playing out of time."},{"type":"prose","html":"Global <strong>Stop</strong> stops transport and therefore stops the tick. <strong>Stop Clip</strong> on the last playing clip can also idle transport. The switch can stay on while you stop. Next Play or launch starts the click again."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Turn the <strong>Metronome</strong> switch on in <strong>Set Settings</strong>.","With transport stopped, listen. Confirm you are not hearing a free-running click.","Tap global <strong>Play</strong>. Confirm the metronome ticks. Tap <strong>Stop</strong>.","Launch a clip (or an empty clip if that starts transport). Confirm the click starts with launch.","Turn the switch off, tap <strong>Play</strong>, and confirm there is no click. Recite the two-part rule."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does the metronome start ticking? <strong>Answer:</strong> After you enable its switch, then press Play or launch a clip.","Does the switch alone start the click? <strong>Answer:</strong> No.","What stops the tick? <strong>Answer:</strong> Stopping transport (global Stop, or otherwise ending playback). The switch can remain on."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.1 Metronome (switch, then Play or launch a clip)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (clip launch); §4.5 (Play/Stop)","Ableton Note Manual §5.2 Tempo (click follows Set tempo)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Do four bars with click + Play, stop, four bars with click + clip launch, stop, four bars with switch off. Write which start method you will use when you Capture in Level 5."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-1-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-1-4","title":"3.1.4 — Note 2.x: empty Set no longer auto-enables metronome; count-in behavior","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.4 — Note 2.x: empty Set no longer auto-enables metronome; count-in behavior","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.1 — Metronome</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+ you expect an empty Set not to auto-enable the metronome, and you listen for a count-in instead."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1: in an empty Set, the metronome will no longer turn on automatically. Instead a count-in will play. That is the whole official change. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"\"No longer\" means older versions did auto-enable the metronome on an empty Set. If you are below 2.0.1, you may still see the click arm itself on a new empty Set. Look at the switch. Do not assume."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not state the count-in length (one bar, two bars, or another value). Do not invent a number. Teach that a count-in plays on 2.0.1+ empty Sets instead of auto-enabling the metronome. After that, the §5.1 rule still holds: the metronome ticks when its switch is on and you press Play or launch a clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"An empty Set means no clips yet. Once you add clips, you are not in the \"empty Set\" case. Use the switch on purpose from then on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong> and write the version.","Create a new Set with <strong>+ New</strong>. Do not add clips.","Open <strong>Set Settings</strong> and look at the <strong>Metronome</strong> switch. On 2.0.1+, expect it not to have auto-enabled.","Tap <strong>Play</strong>. Listen for a count-in (2.0.1+) rather than a click that was already armed.","Recite the 2.0.1 rule and the gap: count-in length is not documented."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What changed for empty Sets in Note 2.0.1? <strong>Answer:</strong> The metronome no longer turns on automatically. A count-in plays instead.","Do sources say how long the count-in is? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is a gap.","After the Set has clips, what starts the metronome? <strong>Answer:</strong> Switch on, then Play or clip launch (Manual §5.1)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1: empty Set metronome no longer auto-on; count-in plays instead","Ableton Note Manual §5.1 Metronome (switch + Play or clip launch)","Note FAQ: check version in Settings → Help"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Make one new empty Set on 2.0.1+ and write what you heard on first Play (count-in vs armed click). Compare with an older-version device if you have one."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-1-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-1-5","title":"3.1.5 — When to disable the metronome","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.1.5 — When to disable the metronome","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.1 — Metronome</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can turn the metronome off on purpose when you no longer need the click to stay in tempo."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The manual gives one job for the metronome: the easiest way to record in sync with a set tempo. That implies the click is a recording aid. The switch is how you remove it."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not list musical cases for disabling the click (\"for feel,\" \"for bounce,\" \"after the first take\"). Do not invent a style guide. Teach the sourced jobs:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>On</strong> when you need a tick to play in time — first capture, stacking a part against drums, checking tempo.","<strong>Off</strong> when you want to hear the sketch without the tick — judging whether the idea is memorable (Beatmaking: give space between create and judge), or when you already have drums carrying the pulse."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, an empty Set will not auto-enable the metronome; a count-in plays instead. You still own the switch once you start working. Export Audio records the scenes, not a separate \"click track\" setting in the sources. {type:'gap'} Sources do not state whether an armed metronome is included in the export. If you do not want a click in a bounce, turn the switch off before you export (Level 11 will go deeper)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Arm the metronome. Play a scene with drums. Hear click + drums together.","Turn the switch off. Play the same scene. Hear drums as the pulse.","Turn it on again and play a scene that is drums+bass only. Decide which listen you need for the next capture.","Leave the switch off and sit with the sketch for a minute (Beatmaking: space before you judge).","Recite: click is for recording in sync; switch off to hear the Set; no official \"feel\" recipe beyond that."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the official reason to use the metronome? <strong>Answer:</strong> It is the easiest way to record in sync with a set tempo.","Do sources list when to kill the click for feel? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is a gap. Use on for sync, off to hear the sketch.","What should you do if you do not want a click in a listen or a later bounce? <strong>Answer:</strong> Turn the Metronome switch off. Sources do not document click-in-export behavior."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.1 Metronome","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (empty Set / count-in)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: space between create and judge; drums as pulse","Ableton Note Manual §5.4 / §10.2 Export Audio (scenes; no click-in-export detail)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capture is Level 5. For now, play your lab Set once with click and once without. Write which listen you will use when you record the next part."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-1-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-3-2-102","code":"3.2","title":"3.2","blurb":"3.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l3-2-1","title":"3.2.1 — Coarse horizontal slider","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.1 — Coarse horizontal slider","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.2 — Tempo three ways</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can set tempo in large steps by dragging the horizontal slider on the Tempo page."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Select <strong>Tempo</strong> in <strong>Set Settings</strong> to access the Set's tempo settings. The manual lists three ways to adjust tempo. This lesson is the first: drag the slider horizontally to make coarse tempo adjustments."},{"type":"prose","html":"Coarse means large jumps. Use the slider when you want a new neighborhood — slower ballad-like, mid, or faster — not when you need 121 instead of 120. Fine ±1 arrows are the next lesson. Tap Tempo is the lesson after that."},{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ says you set tempo manually by tapping the Set Settings button in the top right corner. The slider is that manual path. The other FAQ path — play a phrase with transport stopped and Capture extracts tempo — is Module 3.2.4–3.2.5. Do not mix them. Slider = you choose the number. First Capture with transport stopped = Note chooses from your playing."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tempo is per Set. On Note 2.0.1+, the last used tempo is remembered when you create a new Set. That is later in this module. For now, open <strong>Tempo</strong> and drag."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings → Tempo</strong>.","Note the current BPM.","Drag the slider left to a clearly slower tempo. Arm the metronome and tap <strong>Play</strong>. Listen.","Drag the slider right to a clearly faster tempo. Listen again. Stop.","Recite: horizontal slider = coarse tempo; Set Settings → Tempo."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is the coarse tempo control? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Settings → Tempo. Drag the slider horizontally.","What is this slider for, versus the arrows? <strong>Answer:</strong> Coarse (large) adjustments. Arrows are ±1 BPM.","Is dragging the slider the same as first-Capture tempo extract? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The slider is manual. Capture extract happens when transport is stopped and you capture the first clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.2 Tempo (drag slider horizontally for coarse adjustments)","Note FAQ p.4: set tempo manually from the top-right Set Settings button","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: last tempo remembered (later lesson)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Park the slider at a slow tempo, then a mid tempo, then a fast tempo. Do not worry about exact integers yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-2-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-2-2","title":"3.2.2 — Fine ±1 BPM arrows","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.2 — Fine ±1 BPM arrows","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.2 — Tempo three ways</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can nudge tempo by exactly 1 BPM using the arrows at each end of the slider."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On the <strong>Tempo</strong> page, tap the arrows at the left and right sides of the slider to make fine tempo adjustments of 1 BPM per tap. That is the official fine control. Left is slower by one. Right is faster by one."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use the arrows when the slider got you close and you need a specific integer. The lab in 3.2.7 asks for 90, 120, and 140. The slider gets you near. The arrows land on the number. {type:'gap'} Sources do not publish the slider's BPM range or whether it snaps. If the slider overshoots, finish with arrows."},{"type":"prose","html":"One tap = 1 BPM. There is no documented \"hold to accelerate\" on these arrows. Tap repeatedly. Watch the number."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tempo still belongs to the Set. Changing it moves the metronome and all clips with the Set. On 2.0.1+ audio tracks, loops are warped so they follow tempo without changing pitch. On Sampler/Drum Rack, the FAQ warp rule still applies to samples — that is not this control. This control is the Set BPM."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings → Tempo</strong>. Read the current integer.","Tap the right arrow five times. Confirm the number rose by 5.","Tap the left arrow five times. Confirm you are back.","Use the slider to get near a number, then arrows to land exactly on it.","Recite: arrows = ±1 BPM per tap, left and right of the slider."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How much does one arrow tap change tempo? <strong>Answer:</strong> 1 BPM.","Where are the arrows? <strong>Answer:</strong> At the left and right sides of the Tempo slider.","When do you use arrows instead of the slider? <strong>Answer:</strong> For fine, exact integers after a coarse drag."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.2 Tempo (arrows; 1 BPM per tap)","Note FAQ p.4: manual tempo via Set Settings","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: audio-track warp vs FAQ Sampler/Drum Rack path (do not blend)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"From wherever you are, land exactly on 100 BPM with arrows only. Then land on 101. You will use this for 90 / 120 / 140 later."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-2-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-2-3","title":"3.2.3 — Tap Tempo (4 taps then continuous)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.3 — Tap Tempo (4 taps then continuous)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.2 — Tempo three ways</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can set tempo by tapping <strong>Tap Tempo</strong>: four taps to set it, then more taps to keep updating."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On the <strong>Tempo</strong> page, tap the <strong>Tap Tempo</strong> button. After four initial taps, the tempo will be set based on the speed of your taps. If you continue tapping beyond those four beats, the tempo will update continuously to match the speed of your taps."},{"type":"prose","html":"Four taps is the threshold. Three taps is not enough to set it. The fifth tap and after keep the number moving. Stop tapping when the BPM matches the pulse you feel."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is still a manual tempo method, not Capture extract. You are tapping a button in Set Settings. You are not playing pads. Use Tap Tempo when you hear a song or a pulse in your head and want the Set to match before you play."},{"type":"prose","html":"Tap steadily. Uneven taps will pull the number around as it updates continuously. If you overshoot, you can finish with the ±1 arrows."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings → Tempo</strong>. Find <strong>Tap Tempo</strong>.","Tap it four times at a slow pulse. Read the BPM after the fourth tap.","Keep tapping at a faster pulse. Confirm the number updates continuously.","Stop tapping. Nudge ±1 if you need a clean integer.","Recite: four taps set tempo; further taps update continuously."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["How many taps set the tempo? <strong>Answer:</strong> Four initial taps.","What happens if you keep tapping after that? <strong>Answer:</strong> The tempo updates continuously to match your tap speed.","Is Tap Tempo the same as first-Capture tempo extract? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Tap Tempo is a button in Set Settings. Capture extract uses a played phrase with transport stopped."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.2 Tempo (Tap Tempo; four taps; then continuous update)","Note FAQ p.4: manual tempo via Set Settings (this is one of the three manual ways)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Tap Tempo along with a song you know (headphones, not Bluetooth if you will also play pads). Then check the slider. Write the BPM you landed on."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-2-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-2-4","title":"3.2.4 — First Capture sets tempo when transport stopped","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.4 — First Capture sets tempo when transport stopped","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.2 — Tempo three ways</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You know that the first capture with transport stopped sets the Set tempo from your performance."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If Note's transport is stopped, and you are capturing a clip for the first time, the Set's tempo will be automatically set based on your performance. The manual states that rule twice: once for Fixed Length Capture (§8.1.1) and once for Free Length Capture (§8.1.2). The Capture KB repeats it: when capturing a clip for the first time, if the transport is stopped, the Set's tempo will be set automatically based on your performance."},{"type":"prose","html":"All three conditions matter:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Transport stopped.</strong> If Play is running, this extract does not apply the same way.","<strong>First clip you capture.</strong> Later captures do not reset tempo by this rule.","<strong>You actually capture.</strong> Playing pads without Capture/Add does not lock the BPM."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"This is why you set a tempo on purpose (slider, arrows, Tap Tempo) <em>before</em> a first stopped-transport capture if you already know the number. If you want Note to hear you instead, leave transport stopped and play the first idea. Module 3.2.5 is the FAQ wording of the same fact."},{"type":"prose","html":"Capture Timeout (Preferences) only clears the unused buffer. It does not change this first-capture tempo rule."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create a new Set. Confirm transport is stopped (global <strong>Stop</strong>).","Open <strong>Set Settings → Tempo</strong> and write the BPM before you play.","In Instrument View, play a phrase at a different pulse than that BPM, then capture it (Fixed Length <strong>Add</strong>, or Free Length <strong>Capture</strong>). Stay stopped until you capture.","Reopen <strong>Tempo</strong>. Confirm the number changed to match the performance.","Recite the three conditions: transport stopped, first capture, then Add/Capture."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When does Note set tempo from your playing? <strong>Answer:</strong> When transport is stopped and you capture a clip for the first time.","Does the rule apply to both Fixed Length and Free Length? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Manual §8.1.1 and §8.1.2 both state it.","Will a second capture rewrite tempo the same way? <strong>Answer:</strong> The rule is for the first captured clip with transport stopped. Later clips are not described as resetting tempo."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1 Fixed Length Capture (first capture, transport stopped, tempo from performance)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.2 Free Length Capture (same rule)","Capture in Note KB: first clip, transport stopped, tempo set automatically","Ableton Note Manual §5.2 (the three <em>manual</em> tempo methods — different path)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On a throwaway Set, let the first stopped-transport capture set tempo. Write old BPM → new BPM. Delete the Set if you do not want it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-2-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-2-5","title":"3.2.5 — play phrase + Capture extracts tempo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.5 — play phrase + Capture extracts tempo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.2 — Tempo three ways</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can use the FAQ method: with transport stopped, play a phrase and Capture, and Note extracts the tempo."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ asks \"How do I select the tempo of a Set?\" and gives two answers. First: set the tempo manually from the Set Settings button in the top right (slider, arrows, Tap Tempo). Second: with the transport stopped, play a phrase and add the clip via Capture. Note will automatically extract the tempo."},{"type":"prose","html":"That second sentence is the same fact as Manual §8.1.1–8.1.2, in FAQ words. Teach both so you can answer either way. \"Play a phrase\" means play the pads. \"Add the clip via Capture\" means <strong>Capture</strong> (Free Length) or the Fixed Length <strong>Add</strong> path that creates the first clip. Transport must be stopped."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use this when you already feel a pulse in your hands and you do not want to guess a number first. Play the phrase in time with yourself. If your playing is uneven, the extract will be uneven. You can still correct with ±1 arrows after the extract."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you already set 120 on purpose, do not do a first stopped-transport capture unless you want 120 replaced. Start transport first, or set tempo after the capture."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["New Set. Transport stopped. Do not touch the Tempo slider.","Open a melodic or drum track. Play a clear, steady phrase (even four-on-the-floor kicks help).","Capture the phrase (<strong>Capture</strong> or Fixed Length <strong>Add</strong>).","Open <strong>Set Settings → Tempo</strong>. Read the extracted BPM.","Recite the FAQ sentence from memory: transport stopped, play a phrase, Capture extracts tempo."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the FAQ's two ways to select tempo? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set it manually in Set Settings, or with transport stopped play a phrase and Capture so Note extracts tempo.","Must transport be stopped for the extract? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes.","Can you correct the extracted number? <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Use the slider, ±1 arrows, or Tap Tempo afterward."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ p.4: How do I select the tempo of a Set? (manual Settings, or stopped transport + phrase + Capture)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1–8.1.2 (first capture, transport stopped)","Capture in Note KB (same first-capture rule)","Ableton Note Manual §5.2 (manual three ways)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Extract tempo from a phrase you clap as pads. Write the BPM. Then nudge it to the nearest number you actually want."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-2-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-2-6","title":"3.2.6 — Remember last tempo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.6 — Remember last tempo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.2 — Tempo three ways</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+ you expect a new Set to remember the last tempo (and last key) you used."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1: last used key and tempo are remembered when creating a new Set. That is a version-gated default. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you parked the previous Set at 140 and you are on 2.0.1+, <strong>+ New</strong> should open near 140, not at an anonymous factory BPM. The same note says C Major is the default key for new Sets, and last key is remembered. Those two key facts sit together: C Major is the starting default; after you change key, new Sets remember what you used. Tempo has the remember behavior in the same bullet."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not say whether a first stopped-transport Capture still overwrites that remembered tempo. The first-capture rule is still official. If you need the remembered 140 to survive, either start transport before the first capture or set 140 again after."},{"type":"prose","html":"Below 2.0.1, new Sets do not have this remember behavior. Set tempo every time."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. If you are below 2.0.1, write \"remember last tempo is 2.0.1+\" and set tempo manually on each new Set.","On 2.0.1+, set the current Set to a distinctive BPM with arrows (for example 94).","Return to the library. Tap <strong>+ New</strong>.","Open <strong>Set Settings → Tempo</strong>. Confirm the new Set shows that last tempo.","Recite: 2.0.1+ remembers last tempo and last key on new Sets."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version remembers last tempo on a new Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 2.0.1.","What else does that bullet remember? <strong>Answer:</strong> Last used key.","Does remembering tempo cancel first-Capture extract? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sources do not say. First capture with transport stopped still sets tempo from the performance. Protect a remembered BPM if you need it."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1: last used key and tempo remembered when creating a new set; C Major default key","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1–8.1.2 and Note FAQ p.4 (first Capture / phrase extract)","Ableton Note Manual §5.2 (manual tempo)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, set 94, create a new Set, and write the Tempo you see. Then decide whether your next first capture is allowed to replace it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-2-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-2-7","title":"3.2.7 — set 90 / 120 / 140 deliberately","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.2.7 — set 90 / 120 / 140 deliberately","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.2 — Tempo three ways</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can land the Set on 90, 120, and 140 BPM using the three manual tempo controls."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lab is only about hitting three integers on purpose. {type:'gap'} Sources do not assign genres to 90, 120, or 140. Do not say \"90 is hip-hop\" or \"140 is drum and bass.\" The numbers are practice targets."},{"type":"prose","html":"You now have three manual methods (Manual §5.2):"},{"type":"list","items":["Coarse horizontal slider — get near the number.","±1 arrows — land on 90, 120, or 140 exactly.","<strong>Tap Tempo</strong> — four taps to set, then continuous update; finish with arrows if the tap is 119 or 141."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"You also have the extract method (FAQ + §8.1): first capture, transport stopped, Note sets tempo from the phrase. Do <strong>not</strong> use extract for this lab. Extract would replace your deliberate number."},{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, the last of these three numbers will be remembered on the next new Set. Fine if you want that. If you do not, set tempo again after <strong>+ New</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Arm the metronome and listen at each target so the number is not only visual."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings → Tempo</strong>. Drag near 90. Tap arrows until the display is <strong>90</strong>. Play the click. Stop.","Drag near 120. Arrows to <strong>120</strong>. Play the click. Stop.","Use <strong>Tap Tempo</strong> toward 140. Finish with arrows so the display is <strong>140</strong>. Play the click. Stop.","Write the method you trusted most for each number.","Recite: 90 / 120 / 140 are lab targets, not genre rules; use slider, arrows, Tap Tempo; not first-Capture extract."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Name the three manual tempo methods. <strong>Answer:</strong> Coarse slider, ±1 arrows, Tap Tempo (4 taps then continuous).","Why not use first Capture to hit 120 in this lab? <strong>Answer:</strong> First stopped-transport capture sets tempo from the performance and would replace your chosen number.","Do 90 / 120 / 140 mean specific genres in the sources? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is a gap. They are practice integers."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.2 Tempo (slider, arrows, Tap Tempo)","Note FAQ p.4 (manual Settings vs Capture extract)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1–8.1.2 (first capture / transport stopped)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (remember last tempo)","Beatmaking: do not invent BPM genre tables"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Hit 90, 120, and 140 once each in under five minutes. Leave the Set on the tempo you will actually sketch at next."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-2-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-3-3-103","code":"3.3","title":"3.3","blurb":"3.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l3-3-1","title":"3.3.1 — In Key is off by default (chromatic)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.1 — In Key is off by default (chromatic)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You know <strong>In Key</strong> is off by default, so pads can play all twelve chromatic notes until you enable it."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Select <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> in <strong>Set Settings</strong> to unfold the submenu. The <strong>In Key</strong> switch is turned off by default, allowing you to play all twelve notes in a Western chromatic scale in Note's Pad View."},{"type":"prose","html":"Off means chromatic. Every semitone is available. You can play notes that are outside a major or minor scale. That is useful for blues inflections, leading tones, or drum-unrelated pitches. It is also how you play a \"wrong\" note on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+, C Major is the default <em>key</em> for new Sets, and last key is remembered. That is the stored key/scale pair. It does not cancel the manual's default for the <strong>In Key</strong> switch. Teach both: the switch starts off (chromatic pads); 2.x still names C Major as the default key for new Sets. Look at the switch. Do not assume pads are locked to C Major until <strong>In Key</strong> is on."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drum Rack pads are drum hits, not a scale. <strong>In Key</strong> snaps <em>melodic</em> instrument pads. Drum tracks stay drum tracks."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings → Key &amp; Scale</strong>.","Find the <strong>In Key</strong> switch. Confirm it is off (default) unless you already changed it.","Open a melodic track's pads. Play across the grid. Confirm you can reach notes that are not in a simple major scale.","Leave the switch off for this lesson.","Recite: <strong>In Key</strong> off by default = twelve chromatic notes on melodic pads."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the default state of In Key? <strong>Answer:</strong> Off. Pads play a Western chromatic scale (all twelve notes).","Does a 2.0.1 C Major default force In Key on? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sources do not say the switch is forced on. C Major is the default key; In Key is off by default in the manual. Check the switch.","Do Drum Rack pads snap to the scale when In Key is discussed? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Key snaps the pad layout for melodic instruments. Drums are not that layout."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3 Key &amp; Scale (In Key off by default; twelve chromatic notes)","Note FAQ p.4: Key and Scale from the top-right Settings menu","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: C Major default key; last key remembered","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (melodic pads vs Drum Rack)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With In Key off, play a short chromatic run on a melodic track. You will lock the same pads to a scale in the next lessons."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-2","title":"3.3.2 — Enable In Key; choose root","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.2 — Enable In Key; choose root","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can turn <strong>In Key</strong> on and choose the root note for the Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Enable the <strong>In Key</strong> switch and use the key and scale buttons to select any root note and scale type. This lesson is the switch plus the root (the key button). Scale type is the next lesson."},{"type":"prose","html":"The root is the home note. Beatmaking says the 808/bass sets the tuning reference for the root of the song. Pick a root that matches the bass you already have, or pick a root first and write the bass to it. The Set has one key/scale pair. You do not set a different root per track."},{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, new Sets default to C Major and then remember the last key. If you want D as the root, set D here. The next new Set on 2.x will remember that last key."},{"type":"prose","html":"If no melodic instruments are in the Set, the In Key options are greyed out (later lesson). Add or keep at least one melodic or Sampler track before you expect the controls to work."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings → Key &amp; Scale</strong>.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> on.","Use the key buttons to pick a root (start with C if you want the 2.x default, then try another root).","Open a melodic track and play the pads. Hear that the layout now belongs to that root.","Recite: <strong>In Key</strong> on + key buttons = root for the whole Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What two things do you set after enabling In Key? <strong>Answer:</strong> Root note (key) and scale type. This lesson is the root.","Is the root per track or per Set? <strong>Answer:</strong> Per Set. Key &amp; Scale lives in Set Settings.","What if you want a new Set to open in the same key on 2.0.1+? <strong>Answer:</strong> Last used key is remembered when you create a new Set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (enable In Key; key and scale buttons; any root)","Note FAQ p.4: Key and Scale from Set Settings","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: C Major default; last key remembered","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: bass as root-note reference"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Set root to C, play a phrase. Set root to G, play the same pad pattern. Hear the transposition. Leave the root on the one you will use for the melody lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-3","title":"3.3.3 — Choose scale type","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.3 — Choose scale type","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can choose a scale type in <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> after <strong>In Key</strong> is on."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With <strong>In Key</strong> enabled, use the scale buttons to select any scale type of your choosing. The Set then uses that scale with the root you already picked."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not list the available scale types (Major, Minor, Dorian, and so on). Do not invent a menu. Open the scale buttons and read what your version shows. The syllabus must-not is a full modal theory degree. You only need: pick a type, hear it, keep it if it fits the idea."},{"type":"prose","html":"Scale type is per Set, same as the root. Changing Major to another type snaps melodic pads again. Existing captured notes are not described as auto-rewriting. {type:'gap'} Sources do not say captured notes retune when you change scale. New playing follows the new pad layout. Transpose in the MIDI Editor will quantize melodic notes to scale degrees if a key and scale has been set — that is a later-level edit fact, not a live rewrite."},{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, C Major is the default key for new Sets. \"Major\" is a scale type in that default name."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Set Settings → Key &amp; Scale</strong>. Leave <strong>In Key</strong> on and a root selected.","Open the scale-type buttons. Write the names you actually see (do not copy a list from memory of Live).","Pick one type. Play the melodic pads. Pick a second type. Play the same pad pattern.","Return to the type you want for this Set.","Recite: scale type is a Set Settings choice; the official docs do not publish the list."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where do you choose scale type? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Settings → Key &amp; Scale, after In Key is on.","Do the extracts list every scale type? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That is a gap. Read the buttons in your app.","Does changing scale type rewrite notes already captured? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sources do not say they retune. New pads follow the new layout. MIDI Transpose later snaps to scale degrees if key/scale is set."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (scale buttons; any scale type)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.2 Transpose (melodic transpose quantizes to scale degrees if key/scale set)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: C Major default key","Note FAQ p.4: Key and Scale menu"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With one root, compare two scale types on the same pad pattern for one minute. Keep the type you want for Lesson 3.3.9."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-4","title":"3.3.4 — Pad snap behavior for melodic instruments","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.4 — Pad snap behavior for melodic instruments","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can explain that <strong>In Key</strong> snaps the pad layout for melodic instruments to notes in the chosen scale."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When you enable <strong>In Key</strong> and choose a root and scale type, this will snap the pad layout for melodic instruments to notes within the scale. \"Snap the pad layout\" is the official verb. The pads themselves become the scale. You are not filtering after the fact. You cannot hit an out-of-scale pitch from those snapped pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"Melodic instruments means Drift, Wavetable, Melodic Sampler — the tracks whose Pad View is a 5×5 grid (or Keys). Drum Rack stays 4×4 drum hits. Transposing notes in a Drum Rack does not change pitch; it moves hits to different pads. Do not expect drums to snap to C Major."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is why the melody lab works: a scale-locked grid cannot play a \"wrong\" scale degree from the pads. Chromatic passing notes require <strong>In Key</strong> off (or Keys layout with the switch off)."},{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, new melodic tracks default to a piano layout. Piano chromatic versus In-Key rectangles is Lesson 3.3.7. The snap rule is the same: In Key on → only scale notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Turn <strong>In Key</strong> on. Set a root and a scale type.","Open a melodic track. Play every pad you can reach. Listen for a closed scale.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> off. Play again. Confirm extra (chromatic) notes return.","Open a Drum Rack. Confirm those pads did not become scale notes.","Recite: In Key snaps melodic pad layout to the scale; drums do not use that snap."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does In Key do to melodic pads? <strong>Answer:</strong> It snaps the pad layout to notes within the chosen scale.","Can you play an out-of-scale note from those snapped pads? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The layout only contains in-scale notes.","Does this snap apply to Drum Rack? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is for melodic instruments."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (snap pad layout for melodic instruments)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (5×5 melodic pads; 4×4 Drum Rack)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.2 (Drum Rack transpose moves pads, not pitch)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: piano layout default for new melodic tracks"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On one melodic track, record in your head a four-note pattern with In Key on. You will capture it in 3.3.9. Leave In Key on."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-5","title":"3.3.5 — Preview pads in Key & Scale menu","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.5 — Preview pads in Key & Scale menu","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can preview a key and scale inside the <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> menu before you play the Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A set of melodic pads is included in the <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> submenu so you can hear the key and scale settings before using them in the Set. The pad sound preview uses whichever melodic instrument was last selected to generate its preview sound."},{"type":"prose","html":"That means: pick a melodic track first (tap its header) so \"last selected\" is the sound you care about. Then open <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> and use the preview pads. You hear the new root/scale on that instrument without guessing from the names of the buttons."},{"type":"prose","html":"The In Key options will be greyed out if no melodic instruments are present in the Set. Preview pads need a melodic instrument to exist. A Set of only Drum Racks will not give you this preview path."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use preview when you change root and want to hear the bass-friendly home note, or when you flip scale type and want five seconds of evidence before you recapture a melody."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Tap a melodic track header so that instrument is last selected.","Open <strong>Set Settings → Key &amp; Scale</strong>.","Play the preview pads. Change root. Play again. Change scale type. Play again.","Close Set Settings. Play the actual track pads. Confirm they match what you previewed.","Recite: preview pads live in Key &amp; Scale; they use the last selected melodic instrument."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where are the preview pads? <strong>Answer:</strong> In the Key &amp; Scale submenu in Set Settings.","Which sound do they use? <strong>Answer:</strong> Whichever melodic instrument was last selected.","What if the Set has no melodic instruments? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Key options are greyed out. Preview has nothing to generate from."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (preview pads; last selected melodic instrument; greyed if none)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Preview two roots on the last selected lead sound. Pick one. You will write a melody in that key in 3.3.9."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-5</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-6","title":"3.3.6 — Greyed options if no melodic instruments","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.6 — Greyed options if no melodic instruments","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can explain why <strong>In Key</strong> options grey out and how to restore them."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>In Key</strong> options will be greyed out if no melodic instruments are present in the Set. That is the official disable rule. A Set that is only Drum Racks (or only 2.x audio tracks) has no melodic pad layout to snap."},{"type":"prose","html":"\"Melodic instruments\" in the manual means the tracks whose pads follow Key &amp; Scale: Drift, Wavetable, Melodic Sampler. The three default tracks in a new Set usually include at least one of drum, bass, melodic, or Sampler — so a fresh <strong>+ New</strong> Set is unlikely to be fully greyed. You grey the menu by deleting every melodic/Sampler track."},{"type":"prose","html":"To restore the options, add a melodic track. Pre-2.0: swipe left → <strong>Add Track</strong> and load a melodic or Sampler preset. On 2.0.1+: add a <strong>Synth</strong> track. Stay under eight tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"Preview pads in the same menu also depend on a last-selected melodic instrument. If everything is grey, do not hunt a hidden switch. Add a melodic track first."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> on your lab Set (which should have a melodic track). Confirm the options are active.","If you have a spare slot under eight, you may duplicate a Drum Rack only on a throwaway Set and delete melodic tracks to <em>see</em> the grey state. Otherwise, take the manual at its word and do not wreck the lab Set.","Add or keep one Drift / Wavetable / Melodic Sampler track.","Reopen <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong>. Confirm <strong>In Key</strong>, root, scale, and preview pads are available.","Recite: no melodic instruments → In Key options greyed out."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When are In Key options greyed out? <strong>Answer:</strong> When no melodic instruments are present in the Set.","How do you restore them? <strong>Answer:</strong> Add a melodic instrument (Synth / Drift / Wavetable / Melodic Sampler) without exceeding eight tracks.","Why does a new Set usually not start greyed? <strong>Answer:</strong> New Sets start with three tracks preloaded with drum, bass, melodic, or Sampler presets — typically at least one melodic or Sampler."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (greyed if no melodic instruments)","Ableton Note Manual §3.1 / §4.1 (three default tracks; add up to eight)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: Beat / Synth / Audio add buttons"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Confirm your Gate C Set has at least one melodic track so Key &amp; Scale cannot grey out during the melody lab."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-6</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-7","title":"3.3.7 — Keys layout: rectangles when In Key vs piano chromatic when off","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.7 — Keys layout: rectangles when In Key vs piano chromatic when off","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can switch a melodic track to <strong>Keys</strong> and see rectangles when <strong>In Key</strong> is on, or a piano chromatic keyboard when it is off."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Keys</strong> replaces the Pads layout with a two-octave keyboard. If the <strong>In Key</strong> switch is enabled in Key &amp; Scale, the Keys layout appears as a row of identical rectangles. When the switch is turned off, a more familiar piano-style chromatic keyboard appears instead, complete with sharp and flat notes."},{"type":"prose","html":"Those two pictures are the whole lesson. Rectangles = scale degrees, equal width. Piano = chromatic, black and white. The same <strong>In Key</strong> switch that snaps the 5×5 pads also changes Keys."},{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+, piano layout is the default for new melodic tracks. That default is the chromatic piano picture (In Key off, or the 2.x piano default). If you want rectangles, turn <strong>In Key</strong> on and open <strong>Keys</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Layout choice is per track and is saved with the Set. One track can be Keys, another Pads. <strong>In Key</strong> itself is still per Set."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a melodic track. Open <strong>Pad Performance Options</strong> and choose <strong>Keys</strong>.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> on in <strong>Set Settings</strong>. Return to Keys. Confirm a row of identical rectangles.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> off. Return to Keys. Confirm a piano-style chromatic keyboard with sharps and flats.","Switch back to Pads if you prefer. Confirm the track remembers the layout after you leave and reopen.","Recite: Keys + In Key on = rectangles; Keys + In Key off = piano chromatic."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Keys look like when In Key is on? <strong>Answer:</strong> A row of identical rectangles.","What does it look like when In Key is off? <strong>Answer:</strong> A piano-style chromatic keyboard with sharps and flats.","What did Note 2.0.1 change about melodic layout? <strong>Answer:</strong> Piano layout is now the default for new melodic tracks."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 Keys (two-octave keyboard; rectangles vs piano chromatic)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (layout is per track and saved with the Set)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1: piano layout default for new melodic tracks","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (In Key switch)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Play the same four-note idea on rectangles (In Key on) and on the piano keyboard (In Key off). Notice which notes disappear when the layout becomes rectangles."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-7</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-8","title":"3.3.8 — Default C Major on new Sets","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.8 — Default C Major on new Sets","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 2.0.1+ you expect a new Set to start in C Major, then remember the last key you used."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 2.0.1: C Major is the default key for new Sets. The same improvements bullet says last used key and tempo are remembered when creating a new Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"Read those together. C Major is the factory default. After you work in G (or another root/type), <strong>+ New</strong> remembers the last key. You will not always see C Major on every new Set — only until you change key, or on a first launch / reset-like state the notes do not describe further. {type:'gap'} Sources do not say how to restore the C Major factory default except by setting C Major again."},{"type":"prose","html":"The pre-2.0 manual does not name C Major as a default. On older versions, look at <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> every time. The <strong>In Key</strong> switch is still off by default in the manual, so even a 2.x C Major default does not mean the pads are locked until you enable <strong>In Key</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Piano layout as the default for new melodic tracks is a separate 2.0.1 line. Key default ≠ layout default."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. If you are below 2.0.1, write \"C Major default is 2.0.1+\" and skip the remember test.","On 2.0.1+, open <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> on a new Set. Write the key you see (expect C Major if you have not changed key yet).","Change the key to something else. Create another new Set. Confirm last key was remembered.","Set C Major again if you want that as the remembered key.","Recite: 2.0.1+ default key is C Major; last key is remembered; In Key switch is still its own control."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What is the default key for new Sets on 2.0.1+? <strong>Answer:</strong> C Major.","What happens to key when you create the next new Set on 2.0.1+? <strong>Answer:</strong> Last used key is remembered.","Does C Major default turn In Key on? <strong>Answer:</strong> The manual still says In Key is off by default. Check the switch."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 2.0.1: C Major default key; last key and tempo remembered; piano layout default","Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (In Key off by default)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On 2.0.1+, leave the Set in the key you will use for the melody lab. The next new Set should remember it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-8</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-3-9","title":"3.3.9 — write a melody that cannot play wrong notes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.3.9 — write a melody that cannot play wrong notes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.3 — Key &amp; Scale</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can write a short melody on In-Key pads so every pad you hit is in the chosen scale."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lab is the Key &amp; Scale payoff. Enable <strong>In Key</strong>, choose a root and a scale type, keep at least one melodic instrument in the Set, and play only those snapped pads. The layout contains only scale notes, so you cannot play a wrong scale degree from the pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"\"Wrong\" here means out of the scale you chose — not \"unmusical.\" Chromatic color requires <strong>In Key</strong> off. For Gate C, leave it on."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use preview pads in <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> to hear the pair before you play. Use <strong>Keys</strong> rectangles if you want a two-octave scale keyboard, or stay on the 5×5 pads. On 2.0.1+, new melodic tracks may open in piano layout; switch to Pads or turn <strong>In Key</strong> on so the piano becomes rectangles."},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not need a long Capture session. A 1- or 2-bar clip is enough (max 16). If transport is stopped and this is the first capture, tempo will also extract — set tempo first if you already chose 90 / 120 / 140."},{"type":"prose","html":"Beatmaking: a memorable melody and rhythm is the hook. Keep it short."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm a melodic track exists so <strong>Key &amp; Scale</strong> is not greyed.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> on. Choose root and scale type. Preview the pads in the menu.","Play a short melody on the snapped pads or Keys rectangles. Capture 1–2 bars.","Turn <strong>In Key</strong> off briefly and find one note you could not have played. Turn it back on.","Recite: In Key + melodic pads = only scale notes; that is the \"cannot play wrong notes\" lab."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why can this melody not play a wrong scale degree? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Key snaps the melodic pad layout to notes in the scale.","What must be in the Set for the controls to work? <strong>Answer:</strong> At least one melodic instrument. Otherwise In Key options are greyed out.","What happens if this is the first capture and transport is stopped? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note sets tempo from the performance. Set tempo first if you need a chosen BPM."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.3 (In Key; root; scale; snap; preview; greyed if no melodic)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.3 (Keys rectangles vs piano)","Ableton Note Manual §8.1.1–8.1.2 (first capture tempo; Fixed Length 1/2/4/8/16)","Note Release Notes 2.0.1 (C Major; piano default; last key)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: memorable melody for the hook","Note FAQ p.4: Key and Scale; tempo extract"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Keep this In-Key clip. You will need an In-Key melody for Gate C."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-3-9</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-3-4-104","code":"3.4","title":"3.4","blurb":"3.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l3-4-1","title":"3.4.1 — Global Groove Amount in Set Settings","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.1 — Global Groove Amount in Set Settings","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.4 — Groove Amount</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On Note 1.0.15+ you can set global <strong>Groove Amount</strong> from Set Settings."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The FAQ: to adjust groove, from your Note Set, tap the Set Settings menu in the top right corner and select <strong>Groove Amount</strong>. Note 1.0.15 added Global Groove Amount (and per-clip swing). The syllabus writes 1.0.16+. The release note that names the feature is <strong>1.0.15</strong>. Teach 1.0.15. Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Groove Amount</strong> is a Set-level control, same menu as Tempo, Metronome, and Key &amp; Scale. It is not a track slider. One amount applies globally."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not document the range of the amount, the default value, or the groove template name (no \"MPC 16th\" list). Do not invent percentages. Open the control, move it, and listen."},{"type":"prose","html":"The pre-2.0 printed manual chapter 5 does not include Groove Amount. If you cannot find it, you are on a version older than 1.0.15. Skip the gesture and write that."},{"type":"prose","html":"Use groove when a straight quantized hat pattern feels stiff. Beatmaking treats hats as the place you show divisions and style. Groove Amount is the official Set-wide feel control those hats can ride."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. If you are below 1.0.15, write \"Groove Amount not on this version\" and stop.","Open <strong>Set Settings</strong> and select <strong>Groove Amount</strong>.","Play a hat-heavy clip (or a scene with hats). Move <strong>Groove Amount</strong> up from its current value. Listen.","Move it back down. Listen again.","Recite: Set Settings → Groove Amount (1.0.15+); global; FAQ path is top-right menu."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is Groove Amount? <strong>Answer:</strong> Set Settings (top-right menu). Select Groove Amount.","Which version added it? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.15 (not 1.0.16).","Is it per track? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is a global Set setting. Per-clip swing is a separate 1.0.15 line (next lesson)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ p.4: Can I adjust swing or groove? → Set Settings → Groove Amount","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Global Groove Amount + per-clip swing","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: hi-hat as style / divisions"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"With a looping hat pattern, A/B a lower Groove Amount against a higher one for 30 seconds each. Leave it where the hats feel better."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-4-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-4-2","title":"3.4.2 — Per-clip swing options (when present)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.2 — Per-clip swing options (when present)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.4 — Groove Amount</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You know Note 1.0.15 added per-clip swing, and you treat the UI path as a documented gap."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Note 1.0.15 added Global Groove Amount + per-clip swing. Two controls arrived together. The FAQ only documents the global one: <strong>Set Settings → Groove Amount</strong>. {type:'gap'} Sources do not document where per-clip swing lives in the UI (clip long-press, MIDI Editor, clip preview, or another menu). Do not invent a tap path."},{"type":"prose","html":"Teach what is sourced. Global groove is the Set amount. Per-clip swing exists on 1.0.15+ as a clip-level counterpart. If you find a swing control on a clip in your version, use it for one part (hats) while leaving the Set amount lower. If you cannot find it, use only <strong>Groove Amount</strong> and write that the per-clip path is not in the extracts."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not call this Live's groove pool. Note does not document groove templates. \"Limited Groove\" commentary in unofficial writeups is not a product fact for this course."},{"type":"prose","html":"Check <strong>Settings → Help</strong>. Below 1.0.15, neither global nor per-clip swing is in the release notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm you are on 1.0.15 or later.","Set a moderate <strong>Groove Amount</strong> in Set Settings so you have a global baseline.","Long-press a hat clip and open <strong>Edit Clip</strong> / the clip preview. Look for a swing control. If you find one, write where it was. If not, write \"per-clip swing UI not found — gap.\"","Do not invent extra menus. Use global Groove Amount if the clip control is missing.","Recite: 1.0.15 added per-clip swing; FAQ only names global Groove Amount; UI path is a gap."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Which version added per-clip swing? <strong>Answer:</strong> Note 1.0.15, in the same line as Global Groove Amount.","Does the FAQ tell you how to set per-clip swing? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. The FAQ only says Set Settings → Groove Amount.","What do you do if you cannot find a clip swing control? <strong>Answer:</strong> Use global Groove Amount. Mark the per-clip UI as a gap."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Global Groove Amount + per-clip swing","Note FAQ p.4: Groove Amount in Set Settings only","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 / §8.2 (clip edit surfaces — no swing path named)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write one sentence in your Set notes: \"Global groove at ___. Per-clip swing: found at ___ / not found.\""},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-4-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-4-3","title":"3.4.3 — Feel vs quantize relationship","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.3 — Feel vs quantize relationship","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.4 — Groove Amount</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can separate quantization (snap notes to a grid) from Groove Amount (Set-wide feel) without inventing an order of operations."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Quantization and groove are different official controls."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Quantize</strong> lives in Instrument View and the MIDI Editor. The <strong>Note Quantize</strong> button aligns notes to a musical grid. The Quantization tab snaps selected notes to a beat value and lets you specify by how much they are snapped. Note 1.0.15 added Quantisation amount. Tap <strong>OFF</strong> in that tab to return the selection to its original position."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Groove Amount</strong> lives in Set Settings (1.0.15+). It is the FAQ's answer to \"Can I adjust swing or groove?\""},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not state whether groove is applied before or after quantize, or whether a 100% quantize cancels groove. Do not teach a processing graph. Teach the jobs: quantize to fix notes that were not captured on beat; Groove Amount to add Set-wide feel. If a pattern feels both late and stiff, fix timing with quantize first, then add groove — that is a working order, not a documented DSP order."},{"type":"prose","html":"MIDI Editor work is Level 6. Here you only need to know the two doors so you do not use <strong>Groove Amount</strong> as a substitute for Quantize, or Quantize as a substitute for groove."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a hat clip that was captured by hand (slightly imperfect is useful).","In Instrument View, find the <strong>Note Quantize</strong> button. Do not commit a destructive pass unless you want it — know where it is.","Open <strong>Set Settings → Groove Amount</strong> (1.0.15+) and move it while the clip plays.","Write one line: \"Quantize = grid. Groove Amount = Set feel. Order of the two is not documented.\"","Recite that line from memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What does Quantize do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It aligns notes to a musical grid (Instrument View Quantize button / MIDI Editor Quantization tab).","What does Groove Amount do? <strong>Answer:</strong> It is the Set Settings control for groove/swing (FAQ; 1.0.15+).","Do sources say groove is applied after quantize? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. That relationship is a gap."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §6.1 (Note Quantize button)","Ableton Note Manual §8.2.3.3 Quantization Tab (snap; amount; OFF)","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Quantisation amount; Global Groove Amount + per-clip swing","Note FAQ p.4: Groove Amount in Set Settings"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave this clip un-over-quantized if it already feels good. If it is sloppy, you will quantize with intent in Level 6. For now, set Groove Amount by ear."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-4-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-4-4","title":"3.4.4 — same hat pattern, groove 0 vs higher","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.4.4 — same hat pattern, groove 0 vs higher","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.4 — Groove Amount</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can A/B one hat pattern at a low Groove Amount against a higher amount and choose by ear."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This lab uses one clip. Do not duplicate the hats onto a second track. Global <strong>Groove Amount</strong> already applies to the Set. You only move that control."},{"type":"prose","html":"{type:'gap'} Sources do not name \"0\" as a label. Use the lowest amount you can set as the straight pole, and a clearly higher amount as the swung pole. If your UI shows 0, use 0. If it does not, write \"lowest\" and \"higher.\""},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep the hat pattern short (1–2 bars, max 16). Beatmaking: hats are where you skip through note divisions and add style. Groove Amount is the Set-wide feel on that pattern. Do not also change tempo, In Key, or Main FX during the A/B."},{"type":"prose","html":"If you found per-clip swing in 3.4.2, you may A/B that on this clip instead of (or after) the global amount. If you did not find it, global only."},{"type":"prose","html":"On versions below 1.0.15, this lab cannot run. Write the version and skip."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Confirm 1.0.15+ and a looping hat clip.","Set <strong>Groove Amount</strong> as low as it will go. Play four cycles. Listen.","Raise <strong>Groove Amount</strong> clearly. Play four cycles. Listen.","Return to the amount you prefer. Do not \"split the difference\" out of habit — pick one.","Recite: same hat clip, two Groove Amounts; choose by ear; no genre BPM story."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why use one clip, not two hat tracks? <strong>Answer:</strong> Groove Amount is global. Two tracks would mix two parts, not two feels of the same part.","What are the two poles of the A/B? <strong>Answer:</strong> Lowest Groove Amount vs a higher amount (0 if the UI shows it).","What if your version has no Groove Amount? <strong>Answer:</strong> The lab is 1.0.15+. Write the version and skip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Note FAQ p.4: Groove Amount in Set Settings","Note Release Notes 1.0.15: Global Groove Amount + per-clip swing","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: hi-hat style / divisions","Note FAQ: 16-bar clip maximum"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Groove Amount on the winner. You will report this setting in Gate C if your version includes it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-4-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"note-m-3-5-105","code":"3.5","title":"3.5","blurb":"3.5","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"note-l3-5-1","title":"3.5.1 — Export Audio option in Set Settings","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.5.1 — Export Audio option in Set Settings","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.5 — Export Audio entry (preview of L11)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can find <strong>Export Audio</strong> in Set Settings and say what a bounce contains."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In <strong>Set Settings</strong>, <strong>Export Audio</strong> opens audio export settings for the Set. The full bounce workflow is Level 11. This level only locates the option and the rules that affect when you use it."},{"type":"prose","html":"From <strong>Set Settings</strong>, select <strong>Export Audio</strong>. The audio file contains an ordered sequence of all scenes, starting from top to bottom. The longest clip in each scene sets the length of that scene in the recorded file. That is why scene order and sparse-vs-full rows matter. You are not bouncing \"whatever is soloed this second\" as a documented path."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can choose uncompressed <strong>WAV</strong> (48 kHz, 16-bit) or compressed <strong>M4A</strong> (48 kHz, 32-bit), then press <strong>Export</strong>. After render, a preview appears. <strong>Share</strong> sends the file out. <strong>Cancel</strong> returns to Session View."},{"type":"prose","html":"If no clips are present, <strong>Export Audio</strong> is greyed out (next lesson). Cloud and <code>.ablbundle</code> are transfer paths, not this bounce. This button is audio out of Note."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open a Set that already has at least one clip (your lab Set).","Open <strong>Set Settings</strong> and select <strong>Export Audio</strong>.","Read the format choices: <strong>WAV</strong> 48 kHz 16-bit and <strong>M4A</strong> 48 kHz 32-bit. Do not export yet unless you want a test file.","Cancel back to Session View if you opened the renderer by mistake.","Recite: Set Settings → Export Audio; scenes top to bottom; longest clip in each scene sets that scene's length."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Where is Export Audio? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Set Settings.","What does the bounce contain? <strong>Answer:</strong> All scenes in order from top to bottom. The longest clip in a scene sets that scene's length.","What formats does the manual list? <strong>Answer:</strong> WAV 48 kHz 16-bit or M4A 48 kHz 32-bit."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.4 Export Audio","Ableton Note Manual §10.2 Exporting Audio (scene order, longest clip, WAV/M4A, Share/Cancel)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Open Export Audio, read the two formats, and cancel. You will bounce after the Set has structure (3.5.3 / Level 11)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-5-1</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-5-2","title":"3.5.2 — Greyed when no clips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.5.2 — Greyed when no clips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.5 — Export Audio entry (preview of L11)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can predict that <strong>Export Audio</strong> is greyed out when the Set has no clips, and active once clips exist."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If no clips are present in the Set, the <strong>Export Audio</strong> option will be greyed out. That is the official disable. Empty tracks are not enough. Empty scenes are not enough. You need at least one clip."},{"type":"prose","html":"A new Set's empty slot grid has no clips. <strong>Add Clip</strong> creates a clip container. A captured clip is also a clip. Either should lift the grey. {type:'gap'} Sources do not say whether a completely empty clip (notes never added) counts. If Export stays grey after <strong>Add Clip</strong>, capture at least one note and check again."},{"type":"prose","html":"This rule is your early warning. You cannot bounce an idea you have not put into a clip. Cloud upload is a different door (Set Options) and is not unlocked by this button."},{"type":"prose","html":"Greyed Export is not an error. It is an empty Set telling the truth."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create a throwaway new Set. Do not add clips.","Open <strong>Set Settings</strong>. Confirm <strong>Export Audio</strong> is greyed out.","Tap <strong>Add Clip</strong> on one track and capture (or at least create) a clip. Return to <strong>Set Settings</strong>.","Confirm <strong>Export Audio</strong> is no longer greyed.","Recite: no clips → Export Audio greyed out."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["When is Export Audio greyed out? <strong>Answer:</strong> When no clips are present in the Set.","Does having three default tracks enable Export? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. Tracks without clips are not enough.","Is greyed Export a crash or a permissions problem? <strong>Answer:</strong> No. It is the documented empty-Set state."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.4 Export Audio (greyed if no clips)","Ableton Note Manual §4.2 (empty slot grid; Add Clip creates a clip)","Ableton Note Manual §10.2 (export requires the Set's scenes/clips)"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On the throwaway Set, screenshot or write \"grey before clip / active after clip.\" Delete the throwaway Set if you do not need it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-5-2</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-5-3","title":"3.5.3 — Export after the Set has clips and structure","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.5.3 — Export after the Set has clips and structure","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.5 — Export Audio entry (preview of L11)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You wait to bounce until the Set has clips in scene order, because export plays those scenes top to bottom."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Export Audio is available as soon as clips exist. That does not mean you should bounce the first empty clip. The file is an ordered sequence of all scenes from top to bottom. The longest clip in each scene sets that scene's length. If scene 1 is a 2-bar hook and scene 2 is an 8-bar verse, the bounce is those lengths in that order."},{"type":"prose","html":"Structure first: at least one full row and one thinner row (Level 2 labs). Then check mute/solo — export follows clips in scenes, not a documented solo-only mix. Turn the metronome off if you do not want to risk a click in the listen (click-in-export is a gap). Set tempo and key the way you want them heard."},{"type":"prose","html":"Level 11 covers Share, WAV vs M4A choices in real deliveries, and Live hand-off (Cloud, <code>.ablbundle</code>, Collect All and Save). This lesson is the reason to wait: a bounce of one unarranged loop is legal and usually not the idea. Beatmaking: do not spend too long fine-tuning until you decide the idea is worth continuing — but do put the sections in rows before you render."},{"type":"prose","html":"You still have at most eight scenes. Order them with long-press drag before you export."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the lab Set that has ≥2 scenes and ≥1 clip per section you care about.","Show scene numbers. Read the rows from top to bottom. That is the bounce order.","Confirm <strong>Export Audio</strong> is not greyed (clips exist).","If a row is in the wrong place, long-press its scene number and drag it. Do not export yet unless you want a reference bounce.","Recite: wait for clips + scene order; export is top-to-bottom scenes; longest clip in each scene sets length."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Why wait for structure instead of bouncing the first clip? <strong>Answer:</strong> Export concatenates scenes top to bottom. An unarranged grid becomes an unarranged file.","What sets each scene's length in the file? <strong>Answer:</strong> The longest clip in that scene.","Does mute/solo define the bounce, according to the sources? <strong>Answer:</strong> Sources describe scenes and clips. They do not document mute/solo as export inputs. Put the arrangement in the rows."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.4 and §10.2 (Export Audio; scene order; longest clip; greyed if no clips)","Ableton Note Manual §4.3 (scene order; long-press rearrange)","Beatmaking with Ableton Note: sections from a starting loop; 80/20","Note FAQ: 8 scenes; 16-bar clips"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write the bounce order for your lab Set (scene 1 = …, scene 2 = …). Fix the order now. Full export practice is Level 11."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-5-3</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"note-l3-5-4","title":"3.5.4 — Gate C assessment","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"3.5.4 — Gate C assessment","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Level 3 · Module 3.5 — Export Audio entry (preview of L11)</em>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Objective","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"You can self-score Gate C from Level 3 facts: tempo three ways, an In-Key melody, Groove Amount if your version has it, and Export grey/active states."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Instruction","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Gate C is a checklist you score yourself. The academy app cannot watch Note. Use your own Set, not a Demo."},{"type":"prose","html":"Required bar, from this level's sourced facts:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Set Settings.</strong> You open it from the upper-right icon. You can find Metronome, Tempo, Key &amp; Scale, Export Audio, and (1.0.15+) Groove Amount.","<strong>Metronome.</strong> Switch in Set Settings. Tick starts after the switch is on <em>and</em> you press Play or launch a clip. On 2.0.1+, an empty Set does not auto-enable the metronome; a count-in plays instead (length not documented).","<strong>Tempo three ways.</strong> Coarse slider, ±1 arrows, Tap Tempo (four taps, then continuous). Plus the extract rule: first capture with transport stopped sets tempo (manual + FAQ). On 2.0.1+, last tempo is remembered.","<strong>Key &amp; Scale.</strong> In Key off = chromatic. On + root + scale snaps melodic pads. Preview pads in the menu. Greyed if no melodic instruments. Keys = rectangles when In Key is on, piano chromatic when off. 2.0.1+: C Major default; last key remembered.","<strong>Groove.</strong> 1.0.15+ <strong>Groove Amount</strong> in Set Settings. Per-clip swing exists in that release; UI path is a gap. Skip groove if your version is older.","<strong>Export.</strong> <strong>Export Audio</strong> in Set Settings; greyed when no clips; bounce is scenes top to bottom."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Pass means you performed each line. Fail means you repeat the owning lesson, then rescore."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Procedure","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Open the Set you will use for Gate C (clips exist, at least one melodic track).","Walk the checklist below. Write Pass or Fail for each line.","Fix every Fail using the cited lesson. Do not start Level 4 with an open Fail on tempo, In Key, or Export grey/active.","Play the In-Key melody. Then set tempo with slider, arrows, and Tap Tempo once each (or show you already can).","Recite Gate C: tempo three ways, In-Key melody, Groove Amount if available, Export grey vs active."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-check","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["What are the three <em>manual</em> tempo methods, and what is the extract method? <strong>Answer:</strong> Slider, ±1 arrows, Tap Tempo (4 then continuous). Extract: first capture, transport stopped (FAQ + §8.1).","What must be true for an In-Key melody that cannot play wrong scale notes? <strong>Answer:</strong> In Key on, root and scale chosen, at least one melodic instrument, play the snapped pads.","When is Export Audio greyed out? <strong>Answer:</strong> When the Set has no clips."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Note Manual §5.1–5.4; §8.1.1–8.1.3; §10.2","Note FAQ p.4: tempo (manual + extract), Groove Amount, Key and Scale; 8/8/16 limits","Note Release Notes 1.0.15 (Groove Amount, per-clip swing); 2.0.1 (C Major, last key/tempo, empty-Set metronome/count-in, piano default)","Course syllabus Gate C: tempo three ways; In Key melody; groove amount if available"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Write a pass/fail list. Do not open Level 4 until every required line is Pass. Groove is required only if Help ≥ 1.0.15."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Gate C checklist</strong>"},{"type":"table","headers":["Item","Pass?"],"rows":[["Own Set (not a Demo)",""],["Open Set Settings from upper right",""],["Metronome switch on, then Play or clip launch starts the click",""],["Metronome switch off — Set plays without click",""],["2.0.1+: empty-Set / count-in fact stated (if on that version)",""],["Tempo: coarse slider",""],["Tempo: ±1 arrows",""],["Tempo: Tap Tempo (4 taps, then continuous)",""],["First Capture + transport stopped sets tempo (stated or demonstrated on a throwaway)",""],["2.0.1+: last tempo/key remember stated",""],["In Key off = chromatic; In Key on + root + scale",""],["Preview pads used; options not greyed (melodic track present)",""],["Keys rectangles vs piano chromatic demonstrated",""],["Short In-Key melody captured (≤16 bars)",""],["1.0.15+: Groove Amount A/B on hats (skip if older)",""],["Export Audio found; grey with no clips; active with clips",""],["Export is scenes top-to-bottom (stated; full bounce in L11)",""]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Syllabus ID: <code>note-l3-5-4</code></em>"}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
